{
  "id": "COMPOUND:14268 - PROTEIN:2575",
  "verbalization": "Benzocaine is implicated in the regulation of Cyclin-dependent kinase 7 (CDK7) through a multi-step mechanism involving the 4-(3-Pyridin-2-Yl-1h-Pyrazol-4-Yl)Quinoline compound. This pathway is supported by three distinct but interconnected evidence chains, all of which point to a common target of the quinoline compound. The first evidence chain links benzocaine to the Tumor Susceptibility Gene 101 protein, which is targeted by the quinoline compound and ultimately inhibits CDK7. The second evidence chain connects benzocaine to Glutamine--fructose-6-phosphate aminotransferase [isomerizing], which is also targeted by the quinoline compound and similarly inhibits CDK7. The third evidence chain links benzocaine to HSVEC, which is targeted by the quinoline compound and inhibits CDK7. All three evidence chains share a common intermediate, the quinoline compound, which is a known inhibitor of CDK7. The consistent presence of the quinoline compound in all three pathways suggests a robust mechanistic link between benzocaine and CDK7, supported by a shared target and a well-documented mode of action. The evidence is consistent and does not require additional layers of complexity, making it a strong candidate for a mechanistic relationship between benzocaine and CDK7.",
